  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs of 8 PM EDT, band of showers/storms associated with warm \nadvective regime has shifted mainly east of the region. However,\nadditional isolated/scattered showers and thunderstorms across\nNE PA will continue expanding northeast this evening. Additional\nlocally heavy downpours will be possible with these as they\nexpand into the mid Hudson Valley, SE Catskills and NW CT\nregion.\n\nElsewhere, showers/thunderstorms across western NY will\ncontinue tracking east/northeast, reaching the SW Adirondacks\nand western Mohawk Valley later this evening, and possibly as\nfar east as the Capital Region, Schoharie County and Lake George\nregion toward or just after midnight. Although can not rule out\nsome spotty gusty winds with this convection, widespread severe\nweather looks unlikely given loss of diurnal heating.\nPatchy/areas of fog may develop overnight, especially in areas\nwhich received prior locally heavy rainfall.\n\nThe warm sector shifts eastward tomorrow as the main cold front\nand trough axis swings into the Northeast. Given the early \narrival of the cold front pushing into the eastern NY by 15 - 18\nUTC, the highest chance for the warm sector to have enough time\nto destabilize before the strongest forcing arrives look to be \nin areas mainly east of the Hudson River, especially in western \nNew England. The latest Day 2 SPC Outlook continues to place a \nmarginal (level 1 of 5) risk for severe weather in southern \nBerkshire and Litchfield Counties where there is a favorable \noverlap of 500-1000J/kg of SB CAPE and 40-45kts of deep layer \nshear. Latest CAMs shows an initial line of showers developing\nalong the incoming cold front late morning that grows upscale \ninto line segments and storm clusters as it enters western New \nEngland and the more unstable environment mainly after 17-18 \nUTC. Damaging winds will also again be the main hazard through \nlate afternoon before the front finally pushes eastward into \nNew England. Otherwise, humidity will trend downwards behind \nthe cold front from west to east through the day with clearing \nskies.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWe will enjoy a stretch of pleasant and dry weather Thursday \nthrough this weekend as high pressure builds overhead. \nTemperatures trend upwards Friday into the weekend as high \npressure builds off shore promoting southwesterly return flow \nbut we do not expect oppressive heat. Another shortwave \napproaches late this weekend into early next week but there is \nstill uncertainty on timing and placement of any showers or \nconvection.",
